var e=`<p>Standard degrees increasingly carry named specialisations: business analytics inside an MBA, digital marketing inside a BBA, fintech inside a commerce programme. Some are substantial. Some are a label. The difference is checkable.</p>
<h2>The question that separates them</h2>
<p>Does the specialisation appear on the degree certificate and the consolidated marksheet, or only in the brochure?</p>
<p>If it appears on the certificate, it is part of your qualification and an employer&#39;s filter will read it. If it does not, it is something you can describe in an interview but cannot evidence on paper. Both have value; they are not the same value, and the fee is often the same.</p>
<p>Ask this before enrolling, and ask for the answer in writing.</p>
<h2>Checking the substance</h2>
<p>Ask for the paper list with credit weightings. A real specialisation is three or four named papers carrying real credits. A nominal one is a single elective and a changed programme title.</p>
<p>Then ask whether there is a project. A specialisation assessed only by written examination teaches you to describe the field. One with an assessed project makes you work in it, and that is the part worth putting on a CV.</p>
<h2>Choosing one</h2>
<p>Pick against the role you want next, not the subject that sounds most current. Analytics and digital marketing are genuinely useful in commerce and management careers. A specialisation in a field you have no intention of entering is a line on a certificate that never gets read.</p>
<p>Where you are unsure, the general specialisation keeps more doors open than a narrow one aimed at a role you have not committed to.</p>
<h2>Micro-credentials alongside a degree</h2>
<p>Short certified courses taken outside the degree are worth something, particularly for technical skills where currency matters more than credit. They do not substitute for the degree when a written criterion asks for one, and they should not influence the choice of university.</p>
<p>The sensible order is: satisfy the requirement with a recognized degree, choose the specialisation that points where you are going, and add short courses for tools that change faster than any curriculum.</p>
<h2>Before you enrol</h2>
<p>Confirm that the specialisation is named on the certificate, that it is made up of credited papers, and whether a project is included. Those three answers tell you whether you are buying a qualification or a title.</p>
<p>Tell us the role you are aiming at and we will tell you which of our partner universities&#39; programmes carry specialisations that genuinely support it.</p>
`;export{e as html};